WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
Succession needs time, but on a rocky shore, winter waves keep turning boulders

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.920
over. Middle-sized boulders hold the most diverse communities.

3
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.340
Is that because of their size, or because of how often waves turn them?

4
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.570
Wayne Sousa studied a boulder field on the southern California coast.

5
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.570
There, winter storm waves overturn the boulders.

6
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:33.433
Overturning a boulder kills some or all of the algae on its upper face, and opens

7
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.753
space. So each turn sets succession on that surface back.

8
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:43.397
Picture a clock on each upper face, counting time since it was last cleared.

9
00:00:44.697 --> 00:00:48.697
On a fully cleared face, Ulva and barnacles usually settled first.

10
00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:51.037
Red algae followed.

11
00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:56.017
Without another overturning, Gigartina often came to dominate.

12
00:00:57.497 --> 00:00:59.157
Survivors can change that order.

13
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.530
Small boulders are overturned often, so their clocks keep snapping back.

14
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.950
They carry sparse early communities of Ulva and barnacles.

15
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.730
Large boulders rarely turn, and Gigartina takes them over.

16
00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:17.923
Boulders of intermediate size turn now and then.

17
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:22.583
They are caught partway along, and they carry the most diverse communities.

18
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:26.507
But size and overturning travel together.

19
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:29.727
The small rocks are the ones that flip.

20
00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:35.947
A bigger surface might simply hold more species, so the pattern alone cannot say

21
00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:36.947
which matters.

22
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:42.907
So Sousa held some small boulders still, fixed in place so waves could not turn

23
00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:47.727
them. Similar small boulders were left loose nearby, free to roll.

24
00:01:49.027 --> 00:01:51.007
Both sets sat at the same height on the shore.

25
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.060
If size is what matters, the fixed small boulders should stay sparse.

26
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.240
If overturning is what matters, what should they become?

27
00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:13.803
They moved on. Ulva gave way, and red algae, Gigartina among them, came to cover

28
00:02:13.803 --> 00:02:15.003
most of the fixed rocks.

29
00:02:16.323 --> 00:02:18.283
The loose rocks kept their early communities.

30
00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:24.637
The fixed small boulders became as species-rich and diverse as larger,

31
00:02:24.637 --> 00:02:26.397
intermediate-sized boulders.

32
00:02:27.657 --> 00:02:29.717
Their smaller surface did not stop them.

33
00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:36.137
On that shore, how often rocks overturned, not surface area alone, accounted for the

34
00:02:36.137 --> 00:02:37.137
pattern.

35
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:40.997
This test varied one thing, how often a patch is reset.

36
00:02:42.297 --> 00:02:45.957
It did not vary how hard each event hits, and it holds for one shore.

37
00:02:47.357 --> 00:02:49.597
Even there, the season of a census mattered.

38
00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:54.157
In spring, large boulders were no longer poorer in species.

39
00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.350
So disturbance sets succession back, and how often it strikes helps decide how far a

40
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:01.850
patch gets.
